# Children of the Five Winds — Bestiary & The Lost Writer

**Version:** 0.4.1
**Source:** Legend of the Five Rings: Children of the Five Winds (Ch. 4 + Adventure)
**Publisher:** Fantasy Flight Games

---

### Favored Weapons & Gear (Ujik War Pony, continued)

The Ujik war pony is a silhouette 3 creature. It can be ridden by a human-sized (silhouette 2 or lower) rider.

### Unicorn War Dog

With wolf or jackal ancestry and bred to assist in battle, massive Unicorn war dogs serve as sentries, trackers, or guardians, although they are sometimes deployed in battle.

- Keen Tracker: Trade; Physical
- Fearsome Presence: Social; Interpersonal
- Dependent on Humans: Trade; Mental
Choose one Martial or Trade skill. When the Unicorn war dog provides assistance on a check made with this skill, it counts as providing skilled assistance (see page 26 in the core rulebook).

### Wild Desert Horse

Herds of untamed feral horses still roam the steppes and deserts beyond Rokugan's borders. Unlike those descended from domesticated stock, wild horses tend to be shorter and stockily built, and they have a striped or dotted shaggy coat that is shed in summer. Their fight-or-flight instincts are much greater than those of domesticated horses, and they are far more likely to elect "fight."

- Pack Tactics: Martial, Social; Interpersonal
- Tireless Runner: Martial; Physical
- Skittish: Martial; Mental
A wild horse is a silhouette 3 creature with a strong flight response. When performing an Attack action check, it may spend ⚡ in the following way:

- ⚡: Immediately attempt a Movement action after the attack resolves.
When the wild desert horse inflicts a critical strike, if the target fails the check to resist, the target suffers the Dazed condition in addition to the other effects.

This section includes several new NPCs that represent some of the travelers PCs may meet in Khanbulak or outside of Rokugan's borders.

On a journey superficially similar to musha shugyō, Qamarist pilgrims travel to holy sites for spiritual fulfillment and general travel, and to visit family and friends. Most Qamarists undertake pilgrimages at regular intervals, so this NPC profile does not reflect an occupation or permanent way of life. In fact, while it is designed with a Qamarist in mind, it can reflect a person of any religion or nationality; anyone can undertake a pilgrimage as long as they focus on spiritual understanding over trade or travel solely for travel's sake.

- Advantages - Perfect Sense of Direction: Trade; Mental
- Disadvantages - Too Talkative: Social; Infamy

### IMPROVISED TOOLS

When making a Trade skill (Fire) check, the Ujik guide may spend ☘ in the following way:

- Advantages - Daikoku's Blessing: Social; Interpersonal, Spiritual
- Disadvantages - Quarrelsome: Social; Interpersonal

### THE RIGHT PRIZE, THE RIGHT PRICE

Once per scene as an action, the Ujik trader may make a Commerce (Water) check to determine whether an item they have is something another character wants. The TN is equal to item's rarity minus 2, along with other modifiers per GM's discretion.

The world is a vast and strange place, filled with creatures and beings that are beyond most people's imagining.

#### Chained Djinni

ADVERSARY CONFLICT RANK: 5 4

Myriad beings dwell beyond the Empire's borders, some of them more unusual than others. Among the latter are the ephemeral djinn. Mistaken for spirits by those who misunderstand them, djinn are a primordial people with subtle bodies and immense elemental powers. Yet their supernatural nature allowed the ancient Rempet sorcerers to bind them by exploiting their true names. While the practice is banned throughout the Caliphate, many djinn remain enslaved, tied to lamps, rings, or other objects.

### Elemental Being

A chained djinni is an Otherworldly being. They can move vertically, and they ignore the negative effects of physical terrain. As a Support action, they may alter their silhouette to a number between 0 and X, where X is the rank of the ring corresponding to their current stance.

### Invisibility

As a Support action, a djinni may become invisible and impossible to see, or become visible as a body of rolling flames.

### Incredible Power

The djinni's enemies treat the area at range 0–1 of the djinni as Dangerous terrain. The area around the djinni at range 0–3 is Imbalanced (Water and Air), as the kami are fearful of djinn. Additionally, the djinni may attempt any Air or Fire invocation they wish. The being to whom the djinni is bound is immune to this ability's effects.

### Empowered Ifrit

Unlike an ethereal djinni, an ifrit is a djinni with an imposing physical body and animalistic features, such as cattle horns, the face of a dog or jackal, or the eyes and fangs of a snake. Capable of verbal communication and proud of their great powers, an ifrit might reward a human they consider virtuous, but they more often punish those who attract their ire.

- Giant's Strength: Martial; Physical, Spiritual
- Impossible Feats: Martial; Physical, Spiritual
- Flattery Gets You Everywhere: Social; Interpersonal, Mental
- Short Fuse: Social; Mental
Blazing Touch: Range 1–3, Damage 6, Deadliness 6, deals supernatural damage

### Elemental Powers

An ifrit is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 3. They can move vertically, and they ignore the negative effects of physical terrain. Additionally, they have a natural physical resistance value equal to the ring of their current stance.

As a Support action, an ifrit may become invisible and impossible to see, or they can become visible as a humanoid being with animal features.

### Fox Prince

Fox princes are kitsune spirits who have grown old enough to have three or more tails, entitling them to a noble rank within the strange courts of Senkyō. When they gain this rank, many choose to serve Inari, the Fortune of Rice, although others continue to seek their own amusement. Often dwelling near human settlements, fox princes can be benevolent guardians, mischievous tricksters, spirits of vengeance, or anything in between.

Claws and Fangs: Range 0–1, Damage 3, Deadliness 4, Razor-Edged

Kitsune-bi: Range 2–4, Damage 5, Deadliness 3, deals supernatural damage

### Nice Try

Once per scene, after the fox prince has been a target of a successful Attack action, they may choose for the attack to hit an illusion of themself instead. The illusion disappears, the fox prince takes no damage, and they appear within range 2 of their illusory double's former location.

### Shapeshifter

As a Support action, the fox prince can transform into human form (or back again). While in human form, they still cast a fox's shadow. When they become Compromised, their fox tails, ears, and paws appear. While in fox form, they are silhouette 1 and have at least three tails.

### Golden Bird Lord

Golden Bird Lord is the golden-winged "king of the birds." Often appearing as a gigantic kite bird with feathers made of gold, the Golden Bird Lord is associated with courage, honesty, intelligence, and community. Stories tell of an ancient Golden Bird Lord the size of whole nations. The Ujik consider the Golden Bird Lord to be a protector and a guardian of their mountains, a being who opposes all serpents and supposedly also naga.

> **GOLDEN BIRD LORD**
>
> Some travelers claim the Golden Bird Lord can appear as a bare-chested human with a bird's head and wings, but these might be misinterpreted sightings of tengu or of tengu playing a prank.

### Mystical Guardian

A Golden Bird Lord is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 4 who can fly. They can move vertically, and they ignore the negative effects of terrain. Additionally, once per scene, they may perform any Air invocation other than Rise, Air and Wrath of Kaze-no-Kami. The invocation counts as succeeding with three bonus successes and two [symbol] symbols.

### Swoop

When performing an Attack action check, a Golden Bird Lord may spend [symbol] as follows:

- [symbol]: Move 1 range band away from the target per [symbol] spent this way. If the target is human-sized (silhouette 2) or smaller, the Golden Bird Lord may carry it to its destination instead.

### Imposing Marid

The greatest of the djinn, marids were once worshipped as gods. With all the elemental powers of djinn and the physical prowess of ifrits, marids appear as humanoid giants formed from the elements—when they wish to appear at all. So great is their power that they are able to grant wishes, but only the most foolhardy being would attempt to bind them.

A marid is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 4. They can move vertically, and they ignore the negative effects of physical terrain. Additionally, they have a natural physical resistance value equal to the ring of their current stance.

The marid's enemies treat the area within range 0–1 of the marid as Dangerous terrain. The area around the marid at range 0–3 is Imbalanced (Air and Water), as the kami are fearful of djinn. Additionally, the marid may attempt any invocation they wish. If the marid is bound to another being, that being is immune to this ability's effects.

As a Support action, a marid may become invisible and impossible to see, or they may become visible as a giant humanoid made from the element of their current stance.

- Fangs: Range 0–1, Damage 2, Deadliness 7
- Poison Webs: Range 2–3, Damage 3, Deadliness 5, Concealable, deals supernatural damage

### ABLITIES

As a Support action, a jorōgumo can transform into human or spider form. While in spider form, they are silhouette 0. When they become Compromised in their human form,, their long spider legs appear, or their spider fangs protrude from their mouth.

#### ILLUSIONARY WEBS

As a downtime activity, the jorōgumo may weave an illusion out of webs. They may create any scenery they choose, including objects, within range 4 of themself. These illusions are visually and tangibly convincing, even when consumed as food or used as weaponry. They are, however, ultimately fake. Targets may suspect something is not what it seems even if they still believe what their senses tell them. The illusion lasts until it is willingly dropped, or until the jorōgumo becomes Compromised. When dropped, the illusions become Entangling and Obscuring terrain.

Additionally, the jorōgumo knows the invocations False Realm of the Fox Spirits and Vapor of Nightmares.

### Luusyn Khan

This giant water spirit known as the king of serpents, dragon king, or king of the water can be found near a body of fresh water or lazing in a thunderstorm, protecting the purity of lakes and rivers. Seeing the luusyn khan more as a force of nature than a being, most Ujik people steer clear of them. The Ujik endeavor to keep bodies of water unpolluted and as pure as possible to avoid the luusyn khan's wrath. As drinking water is especially precious along the Sand Road, these efforts pair well other tasks that help ensure basic survival.

With their brilliant silver-blue scales and fangs, the luusyn khan resembles an enormous serpent—but one with horns and tufts of blue-green hair around their head. They speak in a thunderous voice and can sometimes be seen illuminated by lightning in a storm cloud or bringing rainbows to the horizon.

- Ethereal Form: Martial, Trade; Physical
- Terrifying Presence: Social, Trade; Fame, Mental, Spiritual
- Proud: Social; Infamy, Interpersonal
- Lightning Strikes: Range 1–4, Damage 7, Deadliness 6, Sacred, deals physical or supernatural damage
- Gear (equipped): Shrouded by storm clouds (Physical 4, Supernatural 5, Sacred)

### Electric Aura

After performing a successful Attack action check, the luusyn khan may spend (symbol) as follows:

- (symbol)+: Characters within range 1 of the luusyn khan must make a TN 4 Fitness check (Earth 5, Void 5). If they fail, they gain the Disoriented condition.

### Spirit of Storm and Stream

A luusyn khan is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 5 that can fly. They can move vertically, and they ignore the negative effects of terrain. Additionally, once per scene, they may perform any Air or Water invocation other than Rise, Air; Ever-Changing Waves; and Rise, Water. The invocation counts as succeeding with three bonus successes and (symbol)(symbol).

### Manticore

Ageless beings that carry a vendetta against all civilizations, manticores are obscure and powerful individuals known from the folklore of the Caliphate. Sustaining themselves on human flesh and delighting in secrets, riddles, and wordplay, they are said to resemble massive hunting cats or birds of prey, but with a human face and the tail of a massive scorpion. However, they can also adopt human form, sowing chaos wherever they go. They seek power and the enslavement of mortals, and they are eager to make pacts with shortsighted sorcerers.

- Expert Manipulator: Social; Interpersonal
- Silent Stalker: Martial; Physical
- Must Honor All Agreements: Martial; Physical
- Obsessed with Riddles: Social; Mental
- Claws and Fangs: Range 1, Damage 5, Deadliness 7, Razor-Edged
- Scorpion Sting: Range 0–3, Damage 2, Deadliness 9, Concealable

### ABLILITIES

### MONSTROUS SHAPESHIFTER

The manticore is an Otherworldly being. As a Support action, they can transform into the form of a human, a giant hunting cat, or a monstrous bird of prey. While in cat form, they have silhouette 3. While in bird form, they have silhouette 2 and can fly. They can use their scorpion tail in any form. When they become Compromised, the manticore is forced into either cat or bird form.

### TERRIBLE SORCERIES

Choose up to three mahō and up to three rituals that the manticore can perform. Add 1 to the manticore's combat conflict rank for each mahō chosen beyond the first.

### Snow Lion

With white fur and a turquoise mane, this being with a round face and a jaw in a perpetual grin can be found in the mountains, leaping from peak to peak. They do not fly, but their feet never touch the ground. The Ujik associate the snow lion with strength, joy, and playfulness. Some who follow Shinseism believe that its roar imparts insights toward Enlightenment. When a seeker of truth climbs one of the highest mountain peaks, that seeker might end up playing tag or fetch with a snow lion—and on occasion, a snow lion might steal such a person's belongings, necessitating a chase, only to disappear seemingly into thin air.

### MYSTICAL BEING

A snow lion is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 3. While they cannot fly vertically, they still ignore the negative effects of terrain. Additionally, once per scene, they may perform a Void shūji. The shūji counts as succeeding with three bonus successes and (icon)(icon).

### ROAR OF EMPTINESS

Once per scene, a snow lion may roar. Everyone within range 5 of the snow lion must succeed on a TN 4 Meditation check (Fire 5, Void 3) or gain the Disoriented condition and suffer 3 strife. If they succeed, they remove 3 strife instead.

### Wind Horse

A wind horse is an Otherworldly being of silhouette 4 and can pass through solid objects along with any riders. Additionally, once per scene, they may perform a Fire kihō. The kihō counts as succeeding with three bonus successes and (paw icons).

The wind horse can't suffer from the Disoriented or Immobilized condition.

## An Investigation in the City of the Rich Frog

The Lost Writer in the City of the Rich Frog is an adventure for the Legend of the Five Rings roleplaying game.. It is designed for a party of six player characters (PCs) of school rank 1, giving them a group rank of 6, and it may be played as a standalone adventure or as part of a group's preexisting campaign. Players may use the premade characters (downloadable for free from edge-studio.net), each of whom has specific connections to the story, or they may use characters they have created themselves. Due to the intrigue-heavy nature of The Lost Writer in the City of the Rich Frog, we recommend that the game master (GM) read through it in its entirety before running it for a group of players. For quick reference, this adventure is structured as follows:

- Part One: Adventure Background Part One provides necessary context regarding the missing writer, Hana-no-Ame, and the events preceding the adventure. It also features background information on the City of the Rich Frog and on the four primary factions within it: the Dragon, Lion, and Unicorn Clans, and the Kaeru rōnin.
- Part Two: Investigation In Part Two, the PCs follow a trail of clues to various locations in the City of the Rich Frog to track down Hana-no-Ame. They speak with important NPCs and engage in some intrigue conflict scenes; they may take part in one or more duels or skirmishes as well. Each scene opens with narration that you may read aloud or paraphrase to set the stage.
- Part Three: Confrontation In Part Three, the PCs meet with the governor of the City of the Rich Frog and ready their allies. In the gardens of the governor's manor, they have their final confrontation and determine the fate of the lost writer.
- Part Four: Resolution In this final section, the players face the aftermath of the final confrontation. They receive their rewards, as determined by their actions, and are given an opportunity to reflect on the adventure and their choices.

## Part One: Adventure Background

### THE CITY OF THE RICH FROG

Located at the junction of the Three Sides River and Drowned Merchant River, the City of the Rich Frog is one of Rokugan's most vital river ports, facilitating trade among its people. To three Great Clans—the Dragon, Lion, and Unicorn—and to the Dragonfly Minor Clan, it is a city of particularly vital strategic and economic importance. Long ago, the City of the Rich Frog was known as the Village of the Rich Frog, and even then, it was one of the largest river ports in Rokugan. For centuries, the Lion and Dragon Clans vied fiercely and violently for control over it. When the Unicorn Clan returned from its self-imposed exile, it laid claim to the port as well. Weary of this infighting, the Emperor declared the City of the Rich Frog an Imperial holding and dispatched an Imperial Governor to run it. Now, it is considered neutral ground. Its governor relies on rōnin deputies instead of clan samurai to maintain law and order within its borders. As the port exhibits no shortage of conflict, several generations of rōnin have served its governors and magistrates. As the years have progressed, these rōnin have claimed the family name Kaeru for themselves, and they accept capable warriors and administrators into their ranks, no matter their past. Despite the moratorium on fighting over the port, the clans still compete for economic advantage. It is common for clans to conspire to learn the nature of their rivals' cargo, then leak the valuable manifests to bandits. The local rōnin are another point of contention: each clan attempts to co-opt rōnin deputies to advance their agendas. The city's rōnin take great care to maintain the appearance of neutrality while playing the different clans off of one another for maximum profit and influence. The City of the Rich Frog has three Great Clan-controlled districts, each with its own facilities and security. The Imperial Governor's manor sits on an island at the center of the river junction, and it is the governor's Imperial agents who collect taxes and issue tariffs.

### Unicorn District

The Unicorn Clan controls the entire western bank of the Three Sides River. Its impressive wharf is made of polished black granite with softwood inlays for traction. Most incoming goods—primarily textiles, lumber, and coal, when available—are immediately moved by wagon deeper into Unicorn lands for storage. The Unicorn maintain a handful of violet-roofed stone buildings, including a single tower at the southern end of the wharf that is tall enough for arrows shot from it to cross the Three Sides River. Here, the venerable Iuchi Shinchiro oversees the Unicorn trade flowing into and out of the city via Ide caravans. He has held his post for over twenty years, and he speaks of passing the post on to one of his seven sons and going on one last great ride.

### Lion District

Along the east bank of the Three Sides River, south of the Drowned Merchant River, lies the Lion's district in the City of the Rich Frog, one of the Lion's most important trade hubs. Here, the Lion trade foodstuffs and clothing for iron and stone, which they use in construction and arms. Young and eager for combat, the ranking Lion Clan samurai is Akodo Minami, who obsesses over security daily. She prays to the Fortunes for a valid cause for sending her army of three thousand across the Drowned Merchant River against Dragonfly soldiers. The Lion Clan has no traditional riverbank wharf or nearby storage facilities. Instead, the riverbanks are marked with a defensive wall that dates from early in the port's violent history. Instead of a wharf, the Lion use a half dozen canal gates. This ingenious canal network uses the river current to move goods to massive storage complexes that straddle the canals farther inland, where workers load and unload goods in the shade.

### Dragon District

As is typical of the Dragon, their presence in the City of the Rich Frog confuses the other clans. They grant use of their potentially profitable riverbank lands to the Dragonfly and instead focus on a strange collection of shrines at the junction of the two rivers. Fifty soldiers garrison near the shrines, as if expecting some threat to emerge from the water. Their leader is the mysterious Tonbo Kuma, a powerful Dragonfly shrine keeper whose family line has overseen the Dragon holdings in the city for as long as anyone can remember.

On the northern and eastern banks of the Drowned Merchant River lies a modest wharf that occasionally sees use by travelers from the Phoenix Clan. Any goods they purchase are floated downriver toward the sea, then shipped up the coast to Phoenix lands. On the north side of the Drowned Merchant River, west of the Phoenix wharf, is a sizable artificial basin with eleven wharves and one open side, forming a dodecagon. Each side corresponds to a month of the year and features identifying animal statuary and iconography. A large lantern is moved one bollard clockwise each sunrise, serving as a communal calendar.

> **Sidebar**
>
> We recommend that only the game master reads beyond this point to avoid players learning too much about the adventure.

### Hana-no-Ame

In recent years, the name of a particular author-poet has become heard with increasing frequency within the literary circles of Rokugan. This author, under the pseudonym Hana-no-Ame (her true name is Shinjo Higuchi; see Shinjo Higuchi, Possessed profile, page 170), arose seemingly from nowhere and has published an impressive collection of novels, poems, and plays in a relatively short amount of time. Many of these works contain thinly veiled political themes and tackle social taboos, most commonly ones that involve a person's duties to their family or lord. As celebrated as she is controversial, Hana-no-Ame has become especially popular within the courts and in the Imperial Capital. While many nobles count themselves great fans of her work, some consider her themes and widespread appeal potential dangers. It is for this reason that she publishes under a false name and keeps her movements and activities a secret to all but her trusted publishing agent and a few powerful friends. Higuchi's latest work-in-progress is a novel for which she has been traveling to conduct research. The story—reportedly about the love between a samurai and a powerful elemental spirit—is said to contain an account of the samurai's struggle between her duty to her daimyō and her desire for the spirit. Though the work has not yet been published, a portion of the manuscript was accidentally leaked to the public while Higuchi was visiting the Imperial Capital, and it quickly caused a minor scandal among the courts. While this work is not Higuchi's most controversial, the samurai it features is of the dutiful Lion Clan; as such, the story has ruffled the feathers of some of the Lion nobility and samurai.

In order to continue her research while allowing the controversy to die down, Higuchi has retreated to the City of the Rich Frog, where her friend Miya Tetsua (see page 174) serves as governor. She has been staying in secret at Tetsua's island manor here, writing and occasionally emerging to conduct some research in the city, though she has not been seen in the city for over a week now. Aside from a couple of individuals whom Higuchi has consulted with or intends to consult—individuals who do not know her by her pen name—she has told no one where she is save her publisher, with whom she has been keeping in contact via letter.

In the course of conducting research for her tale of forbidden samurai-spirit romance, Higuchi recently purchased a foreign talisman from a less-than-reputable artifact dealer. This dealer, having themself inadvertently become mixed up in a foreign artifact smuggling ring plaguing the city, unknowingly sold her a genuine meishōdō (name magic) artifact. Shortly afterward, Higuchi accidentally awakened the talisman's slumbering spirit—an ifrit—who took over her body in a state of panic and confusion.

Governor Tetsua, having noticed that something is wrong with his friend, now finds himself in a difficult situation. If anyone were to discover that Higuchi has been staying in his home, he could become subject to rumors of inappropriate conduct or favoritism. He is also worried about the consequences should Hana-no-Ame's real identity be made public, and he fears that either or both of them might become implicated in the artifact smuggling operation. Seeking a discreet way to handle the situation, he has reached out to his friend Tonbo Kuma and a visiting Crane Clan shugenja, Asahina Nao (see profile on page 173), to help him figure out what is wrong with Higuchi and find a way to solve the problem.

Meanwhile, a couple of weeks have passed since Higuchi's arrival, and rumors have begun to circulate that the famed author Hana-no-Ame recently fled to the City of the Rich Frog and then vanished. Some people are concerned that her disappearance is the result of political maneuverings against her—and that the Lion Clan may be guilty of treachery.

Each pair of premade PCs (available for free at edge-studio.net) have their own reasons for searching the City of the Rich Frog for the missing author. (If any of the premade characters has not been selected by a player, then that character is not joining their partner for this part of the investigation.)

Although none of these pairs of PCs know the others, their investigations are about to converge on the same unassuming Lion district teahouse…

> **RUNNING THIS ADVENTURE**
>
> Whether this is your first time running a Legend of the Five Rings adventure or your fiftieth, it is advisable to keep a few things in mind to help ensure your group's time in the City of the Rich Frog goes smoothly:
> - When a player attempts to solve a conflict by making a check, this adventure may offer one or more suggestions regarding the skills, skill groups, or rings to use. These suggestions cannot encompass every single one of the infinite ways a problem could be tackled. If a player can narratively justify the skill and approach they wish to use, it is a good idea to allow them to do so even if that skill and approach are not among those suggested.
> - Keep an eye on time. While it is virtually always a good idea to invite roleplay and creative problem-solving from your players, if a scene might otherwise run too long, it is okay to cut it short to keep things rolling.
> - Give players opportunities to subvert one another. Each of the premade PCs has their own goals and motivations. Let your players pursue them!

## Part Two: Investigation

### THE TEAHOUSE WITH NO NAME (LION DISTRICT)

As the PCs head toward the Teahouse with No Name, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> **Sidebar**
>
> In the Lion Clan's district within the City of the Rich Frog, an old and renowned teahouse overlooks the banks of the Drowned Merchant River. It has been there since the earliest days, when the city was just the Village of the Rich Frog. This teahouse's ownership has changed hands countless times over the years: so many times, in fact, that its original name has long been lost. The Teahouse with No Name—one of the city's more popular teahouses—is frequented by a wide variety of locals and travelers alike. It is here, at the subject of each of your search's last known location, that you have been drawn in your efforts to learn more.

Whether separately or together, the PCs have arrived at the Teahouse with No Name to gather more information about Hana-no-Ame's disappearance or, in Kitsuki Tsubasa and Tonbo Goro's case, about the same mysterious woman who has been making inquiries about gaijin magic and talismans. Awaiting the arrival of the tea master in the teahouse's sitting room gives the PCs an opportunity to introduce themselves to each other and get acquainted. When the PCs are ready to proceed, a host invites them through a low door into the tea chamber proper. Shortly thereafter, Tea Master Ikoma Kotone (see profile on page 171) arrives and begins the tea ceremony. Over the course of this ceremony, the PCs take part in an intrigue conflict scene in which they attempt to probe the revered tea master about Higuchi's (or the mysterious stranger's) visit in order to learn more about the author's movements and actions.

#### Intentions

Since the PCs all have the same general intentions for this intrigue, the group should have a single social objective: convince the tea master to share what she knows about Hana-no-Ame's visit to the teahouse (or, for the Dragon PCs, Higuchi's visit. It should become clear over the course of this scene that all PCs involved are searching for the same person). The characters do not necessarily need to cooperate with each other,

as all their efforts will funnel them toward the same goal regardless. PC Goal: Appeal to Tea Master Ikoma Kotone. Based on the Appeal to a Person or Group social objective (see page 255 of the core rulebook), any PC or group of PCs attempting this objective must accrue momentum points toward it to succeed via the Persuade action targeting Tea Master Ikoma Kotone. The number of momentum points needed is noted in Momentum.

> **THE TEA CEREMONY**
>
> In Rokugan, tea ceremonies are equal parts performance, social ritual, and spiritual observation. A tea ceremony may serve as a formal gathering, sometimes encompassing a variety of social classes; as a form of entertainment; as a display of status; or as an opportunity for camaraderie. The ceremony contains many steps, each with accompanying ritualistic movements and procedures. While it is not vital to describe them all in detail to your players, you may find it useful to understand the tea ceremony's general flow. In Rokugan, the primary steps are as follows:
> - Arrival: The guests enter the tea chamber, the participant with the highest status first. Guests are treated to a light meal with sake and sweets.
> - Preparation: The tools used in the ceremony are ritualistically prepared and displayed. Conversation is kept to a minimum.
> - Thick Tea: A thick mixture of green tea is prepared in a bowl. Starting with the guest of highest status, the bowl is passed around. Each guest sips from the bowl, rotates it, and passes it along.
> - Thin Tea: Once all the guests have partaken of the thick tea, individual cups of regular thin tea are prepared for them. While the ceremonial tools are put away, the guests are invited to converse and compliment the host.
> - Ending: Once the tools have all been put away, the guests are treated to another round of sweets. They then thank the host, bow, and exit the chamber in the order in which they arrived.

While she is a highly respected professional with years of experience hosting tea ceremonies featuring important or private conversations, Ikoma Kotone nevertheless loves to gossip. She is beside herself knowing that the Hana-no-Ame patronized her teahouse; however, as a professional, she does her best to deflect any attempts by the PCs to prod or ply her for information. PC Goal: Remain professional; do not gossip with customers.

#### Momentum

The PCs have only the length of the tea ceremony in which to appeal to Tea Master Kotone: three rounds at the most. In that time, as a group, they must accumulate at least 5 momentum points (representing their success in overcoming Kotone's reticence) in order to gather any useful information. Kotone continues to conduct the ceremony while the PCs appeal to her, requiring them to walk a fine line between observing traditional decorum and winning the host to their side. This is represented in Kotone's use of her Stir the Leaves ability, which makes it more difficult for PCs who flub the ceremonial steps to gain her approval. Their need to overcome this obstacle should encourage the PCs to work together and possibly foster some unity between the disparate pairs.

#### Resolution

Once the PCs have participated in the "thick tea" step of the ceremony—the main event—Kotone treats them to individual cups of thin tea and sweets and invites them to partake in conversation. If the PCs have accrued 5 or more momentum points, Kotone is more than happy to reveal that not only did Hana-no-Ame indeed attend a ceremony at her teahouse recently, but that she did not attend it alone. She appeared to be meeting with none other than Tonbo Kuma (see profile on page 174), a shrine keeper and the head of the Dragonfly Clan within the city, and she asked them all sorts of questions about spirits, yōkai, and other mystical beings. Dragon PCs, Goro especially, will know that Kuma oversees the Dragonfly's shrine gardens and holds a significant amount of political sway within the city. Other PCs can glean this information with a TN 2 Culture (Fire) check.

If the PCs accrued 10 or more momentum points, or if they accrued 5 or more and now succeed on a TN 4 Courtesy (Water) check, then Kotone adds that Hana-no-Ame asked specifically about spirits and spiritual artifacts from non-Rokugani cultures and locales. She suggests that since the Unicorn are the Rokugani who deal with foreign goods most often, the author's next stop may have been at the Unicorn wharf. Kotone then refers the PCs to a friend of hers who works those docks, a peacekeeper called Kaeru Haya (see profile on page 171).

#### The Investigator

As the PCs exit the teahouse, they are approached by a man who introduces himself as Daidoji Shin (see profile on page 174). He is looking into rumors about the smuggling of dangerous magical artifacts from the Burning Sands west of Rokugan, and he overheard the PCs' conversation. He asks the PCs to let him know anything they may learn about such items being traded or transported as they search for Higuchi. Before making his exit, he also tells them directions to his residence in the Unicorn District in case they need to confer with him or find themselves in need of assistance as they continue their investigation.

Following the scene at the Teahouse with No Name, the PCs may proceed to any location in the city they wish. Depending on what they have learned, they might choose to visit the Dragonfly Clan's shrine gardens to speak with Tonbo Kuma, the Unicorn Clan's wharf to find Kaeru Haya, or Daidoji Shin's lodgings if they need guidance on what to do next.

### The Shrine Gardens (Dragon District)

As the PCs approach the Dragonfly's shrine gardens, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> **Sidebar**
>
> In the Dragon Clan district, the Dragonfly Clan operates a number of shrines that have been built along the riverbanks. Encircling these shrines are beautiful, lavish gardens that are guarded at all times by a contingent of Dragonfly bushi. On a balcony

> **Sidebar**
>
> overlooking one of the gardens, you see a person drinking tea and studying a Go board. As you draw near, they call out and beckon you over, introducing themselves as you take a seat as Tonbo Kuma, leader of the Dragonfly who live in the City of the Rich Frog. Kuma's invitation to sit and join them is welcome and genuine, but the faces of the guards who have kept watch since your approach warn you to watch yourselves.

In this narrative scene, the PCs have the opportunity to converse with Tonbo Kuma to learn more about Kuma's meeting with Higuchi at the Teahouse with No Name. Known for their warm and gregarious nature, Kuma has an extensive network of social connections with various powerful individuals throughout the city. The following information may be useful as you roleplay as Tonbo Kuma:

- The shrine keeper is guarded when the PCs ask about Higuchi, given that Kuma is a good friend of Governor Miya Tetsua (who referred Higuchi to Kuma) and was sworn to secrecy about meeting with Higuchi at the teahouse. All Kuma is willing to divulge initially is that Higuchi requested a meeting to consult with them about some research she was doing; Kuma does not reveal any of the specifics of their conversation.
- The PCs could employ any of several methods to get Kuma to drop their guard. Joining them for a game of Go (a TN 2 Games [Water or Air] check) or attempting to appeal to their sense of compassion or duty (a TN 3 Courtesy [Fire or Earth] check)—especially if the PCs bring up the danger the missing Higuchi may be facing—is particularly effective. If a member of the Dragonfly Clan (especially the PC Tonbo Goro) is present, Kuma is much more willing to talk right from the get-go. Once they have reason to trust the PCs, Kuma has them swear to be discreet and fills them in on the conversation with Higuchi at the teahouse.
- Higuchi sought out Kuma to discuss matters concerning humans' interactions with spirits and to find out what methods might allow a person to contact or otherwise engage with a spirit. As it is exceedingly rare for an individual untrained in the spiritual arts to communicate with a spirit by chance, Kuma advised that the most likely method might be via a talisman or shrine in which a spirit resides.
- Kuma also mentions that Higuchi intended to consult other shugenja in the city as well. Asahina Nao, a Crane Clan shugenja and meishōdō expert currently visiting the area, seemed to greatly pique her interest.
- Kuma was not previously aware of Higuchi's identity as Hana-no-Ame. Revealing this information could benefit the PCs, as Kuma is a quiet supporter of Hana-no-Ame's work.
- Kuma does not know where Higuchi is staying or where she might be currently. As far as they know, the meeting at the teahouse was the only interaction they have had with her. If the PCs ask directly, Kuma reveals that it was Governor Miya Tetsua who suggested that Higuchi get in touch with Kuma.
- If the PCs have not learned that Higuchi's research might have led her to the Unicorn wharf, Kuma may add that Higuchi mentioned planning to pick up a gift for the governor at the Unicorn markets near that wharf, and that this might be a good place to inquire about her whereabouts.

### UNICORN WHARF (UNICORN DISTRICT)

As the PCs approach the Unicorn wharf, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> **Sidebar**
>
> The grand Unicorn Clan wharf lies along the western bank of the Three Sides River. Constructed almost entirely of polished black granite, it sees most of the trade that flows into the city, especially goods from outside Rokugan. In addition to merchant vessels and storage warehouses, the shops of numerous Unicorn merchants line the wharf, drawing an increasing number of visitors to this popular trade district.

In this scene, PCs arrive to investigate the marketplace by the Unicorn district's wharf. Kaeru Akiara is the somewhat distant cousin of Kaeru Haya. Kuma let them know that Higuchi had planned to visit the wharf. This investigation begins as a narrative scene but might progress to a duel or skirmish depending on how the PCs handle its challenges. If the PCs make a general investigation along the wharf, proceed to Dockside Search, page 165. If they instead search specifically for Kaeru Haya, proceed to Find the Peacekeeper, page 165.

### Dockside Search

The docks and nearby markets are bustling as the PCs arrive. If they have come with the goal to simply scope out the dockside district, ask the players to decide what the PCs are doing to gather information. Examples of relevant checks include the following:

Each of these checks has a TN of 3. Successful checks can be rewarded with fragments of useful information, such as the following:

After the PCs have met back up following this investigation, a number of Kaeru family peacekeepers approach them. The peacekeepers have been involved in an ongoing investigation into possible smuggling activity, and the PCs' queries about Higuchi—who was very recently seen asking everyone within earshot about foreign artifacts—have raised a red flag. The peacekeepers ask the party to remove themselves from the Unicorn wharf. However, with a successful TN 3 Command (Earth) check, the PCs convince them to talk; they do not know much about the situation, but they offer to bring the party to their informal captain, Kaeru Haya, to see what she knows (see Find the Peacekeeper). If the PCs fail this check, a fight ensues (see Confrontation). Otherwise, the PCs will be allowed to leave peacefully, though the peacekeepers will prevent their entry to the wharf for the remainder of their stay.

### Find the Peacekeeper

If the PCs search for the rōnin Kaeru Haya at the wharf, they can locate her with a TN 1 Government (Water) check. Haya is amiable and willing to chat, provided the PCs have not stirred up too much trouble beforehand. She admits to having recently spoken with someone claiming to be Hana-no-Ame, but she does not divulge specifics—she was sworn to secrecy, and she takes her vows very seriously. Haya was told that there were a number of people in the city who Hana-no-Ame wished to avoid meeting—dangerous people, possibly, but not the sorts who would lay down their lives in pursuit of her. In no uncertain terms, Haya says she will reveal what she knows only if the PCs prove that their search is not motivated by malice by facing her in a duel (see Confrontation). She will duel them only if they agree to leave the wharf if she wins.

### Confrontation

If the PCs skirmish with the peacekeepers, they face off against Kaeru Haya and enough Kaeru peacekeepers (see profiles on pages 171 and 172) to give the skirmish a combat conflict rank equal to the number of PCs plus any allies with them. The skirmish may end early if Haya suffers the Incapacitated condition—she orders the remaining peacekeepers to stand down and hear the party out. If the PCs duel Kaeru Haya without the other peacekeepers: Have the players select their duelist. The duel's objective is to fight until defeat—in other words, until Haya or the PC duelist suffers the Incapacitated or Unconscious condition. If the PC wins the duel, Haya agrees to tell the party what she knows; if Haya wins, the PCs must abide by their agreement and leave the wharf. If any refuse to do so, more peacekeepers arrive and force them to leave under threat of arrest.

### Resolution

If the PCs win the duel or skirmish, the rōnin divulge what they know: Higuchi visited the markets daily for a couple of weeks, asking nearly everyone she encountered about spirits and foreign talismans. Haya personally intervened, warning Higuchi against that kind of behavior while a smuggling investigation was underway, at which point Higuchi revealed her identity as Hana-no-Ame to her. As Hana-no-Ame, Higuchi has written favorably, even romantically, about the tragic valor of the rōnin. Haya was only too eager to assist, directing Higuchi toward a particular artifact dealer in the markets who might have what she needed. She now guides the PCs to the same dealer (see Curiosity Shop, page 166). If the PCs lose the duel or skirmish, the Kaeru peacekeepers eject them from the Unicorn wharf, requiring them to continue their investigation elsewhere.

Deciphering this faded document requires a successful TN 4 Theology (Water) check. According to the deciphered information, the talisman's spirit must be removed from its current vessel, whether by force or by diplomacy, before the ritual described in the document commences. Then, any person or group may follow the document's instructions to perform a sealing ritual on the subdued spirit, sealing it inside a new vessel. (Upon hearing this, Chiharu offers to sell the PCs a brand-new vessel for just 2 koku—a discount, they assure them, and a safety measure in case the party cannot retrieve the talisman or something else suitable.)

As the PCs set off for Daidoji Shin's lodgings, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> **Sidebar**
>
> Daidoji Shin's home lies on the outskirts of the Unicorn district, among a group of similarly violet-roofed buildings in the heart of the city's commercial center. A thick wall and screened windows isolate the building from the busy streets nearby. Outside, in a neat little garden, two women share a kettle of tea in the midday sun. One, dressed in the elaborate ceremonial robes of a shugenja, sips her tea with a pleasant demeanor. The other, who bears the countenance—and weaponry—of a bushi, seems significantly less happy to be there.

This optional narrative scene is available for any group of PCs who may need a little extra assistance during their investigation. This scene would be most useful for a group of unlucky or curious PCs who missed out on some crucial information, or who would benefit from some NPC muscle or a shugenja who can help them perform rituals. Most other groups can skip this scene without missing out on much.

Daidoji Shin is currently preoccupied with his investigation and is not home. Knowing he might have visitors and not wanting to be a poor host, he has posted his bodyguard, Hiramori Kasami (see profile on page 175), to await the PCs' arrival. Kasami is certain that this is simply an excuse to get out from under her watchful eye, and she is not happy about being made to wait. She knows this is not the PCs' fault, of course, and she is happy to shed insight on Shin's ongoing investigation. Kasami's potential insights include the following:

> **CHIHARU'S INTEL**
>
> If your players are really playing up any rivalry between their clans, you may wish to sweeten the deal for the winner of the intrigue. Depending on which clan wins, you can provide that clan's representative with the following list's leads or red herrings about the other two clans (these statements could be true, or they could just be rumors or even complete fabrications Chiharu has invented—your choice!):
> Unicorn: "I've heard of a few cases of people falsely 'revealing' themselves to be Hana-no-Ame, but did you know that recently some con artists have been pretending to be her publishing personnel? Trying to get their hands on her money, it would seem."
> Lion: "Some of the Lion daimyō aren't happy about Hana-no-Ame's latest series. The young woman seemed afraid to me, like she thought someone was going to try to get her."
> Dragon: "Word is, some fancy Dragon Clan investigators have been on the lookout for someone matching the description of the young woman who purchased the talisman. I hear the Dragonfly are trying to get their hands on some powerful foreign artifacts, and they think she might have them."

### Nao

Joining Kasami is Asahina Nao (see profile on page 173), a well-respected Crane Clan shugenja and personal friend to both Daidoji Shin and Governor Miya Tetsua. She is in the city to visit Shin but has become caught up in the various happenings and now feels obligated to stay and assist. She will gladly help the party if they need any spiritual guidance, and she can assist in deciphering the sealing ritual if the PCs have already visited the Curiosity Shop. Nao's insights include the following:

### The Governor's Manor (Governor's Island)

If PCs try to visit the island without first exhausting their other options, they are turned away from the ferry by the governor's guards, who explain that the ferry is already full or that the governor is not taking visitors until later. Once the PCs have completed their business in the Unicorn district, they become able to access the island; this is a point of no return, so be sure that the party has done all they want in the Unicorn district. When the players are ready to proceed, read or paraphrase the following aloud:

> **Sidebar**
>
> Located on a small island in the middle of the river junction is the resplendent governor's manor. The setting sun reflects off the waters as you take the ferry across, casting the city in pinkish-orange light. You take this time to prepare yourselves for whatever awaits you on the island.

> **A Little Assistance**
>
> If the number of PCs is small, or if they might struggle on an upcoming challenge, Nao or Kasami (or both!) could join the party for the rest of the adventure. Nao will be present for the final scene at the governor's manor, but she could always join the group early and accompany them there.

> **Sidebar**
>
> When you arrive, a pair of guards escorts you inside to a great meeting room. There, seated at a table, are two men: Daidoji Shin, whom you recognize from earlier in the day, and Governor Miya Tetsua himself.

In this narrative scene, the PCs meet with the governor of the City of the Rich Frog. Here, everything comes together as they learn what has happened with Higuchi.

### The Awful Truth

Higuchi, a friend of Tetsua's, has been staying on the island while researching her latest book. Tetsua says he has been noticing some strange behaviors from her for over a week now, but that something has been particularly wrong with her since early this morning. He is afraid that in her search, she may have angered a spirit and become stricken with a curse. Tetsua adds that he has spent the day trying to find someone who can help her quietly, and that for this reason he has called upon Tonbo Kuma and Asahina Nao for help, seeking spiritual wisdom. The PCs may make a TN 2 Theology (Fire) check to assess the situation. On a success, they conclude that Higuchi has been possessed by a spirit. Depending on other information they have obtained in their investigation, they may also realize that Higuchi accidentally purchased a real meishōdō talisman, and that the spirit bound to it is one from the Burning Sands. These revelations give players an extra ⬠on Theology checks against the ifrit spirit. If the PCs' check fails, then Nao comes to the same conclusions, but the party gain the extra ⬠benefit of their successful investigation.

In this part of the adventure, the PCs—having gathered as many clues as they can—attempt to confront Higuchi. They must use the knowledge they have accumulated to fight, entrap, or free the spirit possessing her.

Nao recommends moving Higuchi to the manor's interior garden for the confrontation, while Tetsua ensures that any potential prying eyes or innocent bystanders are sent away from the island. It is up to the PCs to decide how they wish to deal with the errant spirit once they have excised it from Higuchi: Free it? Trap it once again inside a talisman? Banish it for good?

Nao is strongly opposed to freeing the spirit and even refuses to help with the ritual if the party decides to go that route. Tetsua does not care about what happens with the spirit, so long as Higuchi is unharmed. If any players want to play up their PCs' differing opinions on the resolution of the adventure, now may be the last opportunity for them to do so!

When the PCs are ready, they head out to the manor's interior gardens, where Tetsua soon joins them, guiding Higuchi behind him. This scene begins as a narrative scene, but it may become a skirmish; the players ultimately decide how events play out.

Upon her arrival, the author seems disoriented, asking where she is and what is going on in a voice that does not sound entirely her own. She also occasionally speaks in a language the PCs can recognize as a form of Nehiri with a TN 1 Culture (Earth) check. Anyone who passes a TN 2 Theology (Fire) check will recognize the spirit as an ifrit.

The PCs may use a Commune with the Spirits ritual to establish a line of communication with the ifrit, but they must also succeed on a TN 2 Theology (Fire) check to understand and be understood by it (unless they already passed the check to identify the spirit). A subsequent successful TN 3 Courtesy (Water or Air) check can be used to calm or trick the ifrit and encourage it to peacefully exit Higuchi's body, provided the PCs are willing to allow it to go free (something that some characters—especially Nao—have a strong objection to). If the PCs do not promise to let it go free, the ifrit does not agree to leave of its own accord.

The PCs could instead use the Cleansing Rite ritual to drive the ifrit out of Higuchi against its will. Once they release the ifrit, the PCs have three options: attempt to fight and destroy it, try to seal it inside a new vessel, or let it go free. If they choose the first or second option, the PCs must face off against the ifrit (see profile on page 173). To seal the ifrit in a new vessel, they must fend it off while Nao or another shugenja performs the sealing ritual the PCs obtained in the Curiosity Shop. For the ritual to succeed, the shugenja performing it must succeed on three TN 2 Theology (Void) checks on their turns, at which point the ritual takes effect and the ifrit becomes sealed within its new vessel.

It is possible that the PCs might fight Higuchi while she is still possessed (see Shinjo Higuchi, Possessed, page 170). If she dies, the ifrit exits her body and attempts to flee, at which point it can be released, destroyed, or sealed as described earlier in this section.

If Hiramori Kasami is present (see profile on page 175), she can assist with any skirmish that may occur.

> **DUTY VS. ETHICS**
>
> If there were ever an ideal time to play up the ethical dilemma of this adventure, it is now. Just as in Hana-no-Ame's writing, the question of whether a person's obligations or adherence to principles of right and wrong should take precedence comes to the fore at this juncture. It is the PCs' duty to the governor to imprison the ifrit, but some PCs may feel it is wrong to do so. In this final confrontation, they must consider the balance between the city's safety, Higuchi's, and their own, as well as the dilemma of whether it is right to continue keeping a sentient entity imprisoned.

### Part Four: Resolution

Depending on how the PCs choose to approach the final confrontation and deal with the ifrit, this adventure may conclude in any of several ways. If Higuchi survives, she will thank the players for all that they have done to help her. Governor Tetsua will thank them too, and offers a favor for their deeds. If the ifrit is sealed away once again, Nao will offer to buy the vessel from the party for a high price. The PCs may also choose to bring the vessel to the peacekeepers or Kitsuki Investigators for a—slightly less substantial—reward. The period when your group winds down from their victory offers an opportunity for players to reflect on the adventure and for you to elicit some concluding statements from them. Read the following questions aloud; ask the players to consider them and determine what their characters think:

For their part in the adventure, each PC should receive 3 XP plus an extra 1 XP for each hour of play. If any players felt that their character achieved their personal goal for this adventure, you may award them an additional 2 XP.

